<div class="container">
  <h1>Lesson Plan: Surface Area of Right Rectangular Prisms &amp; Cubes</h1>

  <div class="meta-info">
    <p><strong>Grade Level / Subject:</strong> Grade 11 / Mathematics</p>
    <p><strong>Curriculum Framework:</strong> K-12 Curriculum</p>
    <p><strong>Duration:</strong> 60 Minutes</p>
    <p><strong>Teaching Approach:</strong> Evidence-Informed 4As Framework (Explicit Instruction &amp; Cognitive Load Theory)</p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>I. Objectives</h2>
    <p>At the end of the lesson, the students are expected to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cognitive:</strong> Calculate the total surface area of rectangular prisms and cubes using their respective mathematical formulas (<em>SA = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh</em> and <em>SA = 6s²</em>).</li>
      <li><strong>Affective:</strong> Demonstrate perseverance and accuracy when identifying distinct 2D faces of a 3D object during collaborative guided practice.</li>
      <li><strong>Psychomotor:</strong> Construct a 2D net of a rectangular prism to represent and physically verify its total surface area.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>II. Subject Matter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Topic:</strong> Surface Area of Rectangular Prisms and Cubes</li>
      <li><strong>Prerequisite Skills:</strong> Area of rectangles and squares, identifying 3D shape faces</li>
      <li><strong>Materials:</strong> Cardstock/grid paper, scissors, rulers, mini-whiteboards, side-by-side worked example handouts</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>III. Instructional Procedures</h2>

    <h3>A. Activity (Schema Activation &amp; Prerequisite Retrieval) <span class="step-badge">7 Minutes</span></h3>
    <p>Students complete a 3-question mini-quiz on mini-whiteboards to activate prior long-term memory schemas:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Calculate the area of a rectangle with length = 8 cm and width = 5 cm.</li>
      <li>Calculate the area of a square with side length = 4 cm.</li>
      <li>How many total faces does a standard 6-sided die have?</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="evidence-focus">
      <strong>Evidence-Informed Focus:</strong> Low-stakes retrieval verifies foundational skills without overloading working memory.
    </div>

    <h3>B. Analysis (Guided Notice-and-Wonder with Worked Examples) <span class="step-badge">10 Minutes</span></h3>
    <p>Present a pre-unfolded 3D box (a net) alongside a fully solved worked example on the board:</p>
    
<pre class="net-diagram">
       +-----------+
       |   Top     |
 +-----+-----------+-----+-----+
 | Left|  Front    |Right| Back|
 +-----+-----------+-----+-----+
       |  Bottom   |
       +-----------+
</pre>

    <p><strong>Targeted Prompts (Self-Explanation):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>"Notice how opposite faces (Top/Bottom, Front/Back, Left/Right) are identical in area. Why do we multiply each pair by 2 in the formula SA = 2(lw + lh + wh)?"</em></li>
      <li><em>"If all 6 faces of a cube are identical squares of side length s, why does the formula simplify to 6s²?"</em></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="evidence-focus">
      <strong>Evidence-Informed Focus:</strong> Eliminates guess-and-check cognitive load by directing attention to structural patterns.
    </div>

    <h3>C. Abstraction (Explicit Modeling &amp; Dual Coding) <span class="step-badge">15 Minutes</span></h3>
    <p><strong>I Do (Direct Instruction):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Explicitly define <strong>Surface Area</strong> as the total sum of the areas of all 2D outer faces covering a 3D object.</li>
      <li>Model solving a problem step-by-step: A rectangular prism with length = 6 cm, width = 4 cm, and height = 3 cm.</li>
      <li>Write the visual formula decomposition on the board while explaining verbally:</li>
    </ul>
    
    <div class="math-block">
      Front/Back = 2(6 × 3) = 36 cm²<br>
      Top/Bottom = 2(6 × 4) = 48 cm²<br>
      Left/Right = 2(4 × 3) = 24 cm²<br>
      <strong>Total SA = 36 + 48 + 24 = 108 cm²</strong>
    </div>

    <p><strong>We Do (Guided Practice):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Present a parallel problem on the board. Students work in pairs to construct a simple cardstock net, unfold it, and calculate the combined surface area on their mini-whiteboards.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="evidence-focus">
      <strong>Evidence-Informed Focus:</strong> Combines visual nets with step-by-step mathematical procedures (dual coding) to prevent working memory overload.
    </div>

    <h3>D. Application (Faded Scaffolding &amp; Independent Practice) <span class="step-badge">18 Minutes</span></h3>
    <p>Students work independently on a scaffolded problem set featuring faded worked examples:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Level 1 (Faded Example):</strong> Complete missing steps in a half-solved surface area problem.</li>
      <li><strong>Level 2 (Independent Problem):</strong> Calculate the total surface area of a cube with side length <em>s</em> = 7.5 cm.</li>
      <li><strong>Level 3 (Contextual Word Problem):</strong> Calculate the total square meters of cardboard needed to construct a box measuring 0.5 m × 0.3 m × 0.2 m.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>IV. Evaluation <span class="step-badge">10 Minutes</span></h2>
    <p>Students complete an exit slip with two problems:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the surface area of a rectangular prism with length = 10 cm, width = 4 cm, and height = 5 cm.</li>
      <li>A cubic storage box has a side length of 9 cm. Calculate its total surface area.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
